% ============================================================================
\begin{abstract}
% ============================================================================

We present \textbf{dot-jax}, an open-source JAX/Equinox toolbox for
continuous-wave diffuse optical tomography (CW-DOT) and functional
near-infrared spectroscopy (fNIRS). dot-jax reimplements the redbirdpy
finite element forward model as a fully differentiable, JIT-compiled,
GPU-ready pipeline, enabling automatic Jacobian computation through
the complete forward--inverse chain. The toolbox provides: (1)~analytical
CW fluence solutions cross-validated against redbirdpy, (2)~chromophore
extinction tables with Beer--Lambert spectral unmixing, (3)~FEM system
matrix assembly with Robin boundary conditions, (4)~dense and sparse
forward solvers via Lineax, (5)~GCV-optimised Tikhonov image
reconstruction, and (6)~a comprehensive signal processing chain
including motion artifact correction, channel quality control,
short-separation regression, and spatial smoothing.

We validate dot-jax against redbirdpy at every computational layer
(272~tests) and demonstrate the complete pipeline on the ds004569
high-density DOT dataset~\citep{Sherafati2025hddot} from OpenNeuro:
96~sources, 92~detectors, 3{,}684~channels at 750/850\,nm, with
naturalistic movie viewing. Using a standard-space MNI152 atlas head
mesh, we reconstruct oxyhemoglobin (HbO$_2$) concentration images at
1\,Hz and compare to the published NeuroDOT tomographic
reconstructions.

\end{abstract}

% ============================================================================
\section{Introduction}
% ============================================================================

Diffuse optical tomography (DOT) and functional near-infrared
spectroscopy (fNIRS) exploit the relative transparency of biological
tissue to near-infrared light (650--950\,nm) to measure hemodynamic
changes non-invasively~\citep{Jobsis1977noninvasive,
Scholkmann2014review}. In this spectral window, photons penetrate
several centimetres into tissue, scattered by cellular membranes and
mitochondria, absorbed primarily by oxy- and deoxyhemoglobin (HbO$_2$,
Hb), water, and lipids. The wavelength-dependent absorption provides
a spectroscopic contrast mechanism: multi-wavelength measurements
enable separation of chromophore concentration changes via the modified
Beer--Lambert law~\citep{Cope1988system}.

High-density DOT (HD-DOT) arrays with overlapping measurement channels
achieve spatial resolution comparable to
fMRI~\citep{Eggebrecht2014mapping}, while retaining the temporal
resolution, portability, and ecological validity advantages of
optical methods. The combination of dense spatial sampling with
tomographic image reconstruction transforms fNIRS from a surface
mapping technique into a three-dimensional brain imaging modality.

The computational pipeline for DOT --- from photon transport modelling
through finite element assembly to regularised image reconstruction
--- has been implemented in several established packages: TOAST
(Schweiger \& Arridge), NIRFAST~\citep{Dehghani2009nirfast}, and
NeuroDOT~\citep{Eggebrecht2012neurodot}. These tools have enabled
clinical and neuroscience applications but share a common limitation:
they treat the forward model as a black box for gradient computation,
relying on explicit Jacobian assembly via the adjoint method rather
than automatic differentiation. This prevents end-to-end gradient
flow through the complete pipeline and limits integration with
modern machine learning frameworks.

\subsection{Differentiable generative modelling with the JAX ecosystem}

\paragraph{Generative models in neuroimaging.}
The generative modelling approach to neuroimaging inverts a forward
(biophysical) model to infer latent parameters from observed data.
Rather than treating brain images as static maps, generative models
simulate the physical processes that produce the measurements ---
photon transport, hemodynamic coupling, optical signal formation ---
and fit the model parameters to match empirical recordings. In DOT,
this approach is the foundation: the forward model simulates photon
diffusion through tissue, and image reconstruction inverts this model
to recover spatial maps of optical properties from boundary
measurements~\citep{Arridge1999inverse}.

\paragraph{Simulation-based inference and neural differential equations.}
Two developments in machine learning transform what is computationally
feasible for DOT. First, \textbf{simulation-based inference}
(SBI)~\citep{Cranmer2020sbi} replaces likelihood evaluation with
learned neural density estimators trained on simulated data, enabling
Bayesian posterior estimation for models with intractable likelihoods.
For DOT, where the mapping from tissue optical properties to boundary
measurements involves solving a PDE on a complex geometry, SBI offers
a path to uncertainty quantification that traditional methods cannot
provide. Second, \textbf{neural ordinary differential
equations}~\citep{Chen2018neuralode} and physics-informed neural
networks (PINNs)~\citep{Raissi2019pinns} embed differentiable
computation within physical solvers, enabling learned dynamics that
respect the diffusion equation while remaining fully differentiable.

\paragraph{The JAX + Equinox + Lineax stack.}
dot-jax is built on JAX~\citep{Bradbury2018jax} with
Equinox~\citep{Kidger2021equinox} for pytree-based modules and
Lineax for linear solvers --- the same ecosystem developed by
Kidger~\citep{Kidger2022neuralDE} for neural differential equations.
JAX provides \texttt{jax.grad} (automatic differentiation through
arbitrary Python), \texttt{jax.vmap} (automatic vectorisation),
\texttt{jax.jit} (XLA compilation to GPU/TPU), and
\texttt{jax.lax.scan} (efficient sequential computation). The result
is that the entire DOT forward model --- from optical property
specification through FEM assembly and linear solve to detector
measurement extraction --- compiles into a single XLA computation
graph through which gradients flow end-to-end:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:forward}
  \hat{\mathbf{y}} = \mathbf{R}_{\text{det}}^\top
  \bigl(\mathbf{K}(\mu_s') + \mathbf{M}(\mu_a) + \mathbf{C}(R_{\text{eff}})
  \bigr)^{-1}
  \mathbf{R}_{\text{src}}
\end{equation}
%
where $\mathbf{K}$, $\mathbf{M}$, $\mathbf{C}$ are the stiffness,
mass, and Robin boundary matrices, $\mathbf{R}_{\text{src/det}}$ are
barycentric source/detector vectors, and the entire expression is
differentiable with respect to $\mu_a$ and $\mu_s'$ via
\texttt{jax.grad}. This enables gradient-based fitting, automatic
Jacobian computation, and integration with simulation-based inference
frameworks --- capabilities absent from existing DOT software.

% ============================================================================
\section{Dataset}
% ============================================================================

\subsection{OpenNeuro ds004569: high-density DOT naturalistic viewing}

We use the ds004569 dataset~\citep{Sherafati2025hddot} from OpenNeuro,
comprising HD-DOT recordings from 58~healthy adults (aged 18--76)
watching a 10-minute clip from ``The Good, the Bad and the Ugly''
(1966). The dataset provides:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Raw data} in SNIRF~\citep{SNIRF2023spec} and
        NeuroDOT \texttt{.mat} formats, organised following
        BIDS-fNIRS~\citep{Luke2021bids}
  \item \textbf{96 sources, 92 detectors} in a high-density cap
        (BigCap1), sampled at \SI{10}{\hertz}
  \item \textbf{3{,}684 channels} at two wavelengths: 750 and
        \SI{850}{\nano\metre}
  \item \textbf{Source--detector separations} at 1st--4th nearest
        neighbours (13, 30, 39, \SI{47}{\milli\metre})
  \item \textbf{Published NIfTI reconstructions} in Talairach space
        at \SI{3}{\milli\metre} isotropic, \SI{1}{\hertz}, produced by
        NeuroDOT with the ``Amat'' reconstruction algorithm and
        \SI{13}{\milli\metre} spatial smoothing
\end{itemize}

We focus on \textbf{sub-02}, the subject with the most data
(7~sessions, \SI{3.8}{\giga\byte}), using session~01 for pipeline
demonstration.

