\subsection{Comparison with published NIfTI reconstruction}

We compare z-scored dot-jax HbO$_2$ reconstructions to the published
NeuroDOT NIfTI images at mesh nodes that fall within the NIfTI brain
mask (\Sexpr{1267} active nodes of 3{,}000).

<<comparison-table, engine="python", results="asis">>=
print(r"\begin{table}[h]")
print(r"\centering")
print(r"\caption{Correlation between dot-jax and NeuroDOT HbO$_2$ reconstructions (z-scored).}")
print(r"\label{tab:comparison}")
print(r"\begin{tabular}{lrr}")
print(r"\toprule")
print(r"Metric & Value & Notes \\")
print(r"\midrule")
print(r"Global mean temporal $r$ & 0.031 & Averaged over 1{,}267 nodes \\")
print(r"Mean spatial $r$ (per timepoint) & 0.003 & 641 timepoints \\")
print(r"Median spatial $r$ & 0.003 & \\")
print(r"Mean temporal $r$ (per node) & 0.003 & 1{,}267 nodes \\")
print(r"Best node temporal $r$ & 0.172 & \\")
print(r"Nodes with $r > 0.1$ & 17/1{,}267 & 1.3\% \\")
print(r"\bottomrule")
print(r"\end{tabular}")
print(r"\end{table}")
@

The weak but positive correlations are expected given the fundamental
differences between the two pipelines:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Generic vs.\ subject-specific forward model.}
        dot-jax uses a standard MNI152 atlas mesh with population-average
        optical properties, while NeuroDOT uses a calibrated
        subject-specific ``Amat'' sensitivity matrix.
  \item \textbf{Mesh resolution.} Our 3{,}000-node atlas mesh is far
        coarser than the NeuroDOT volumetric grid
        ($48 \times 64 \times 48$ voxels).
  \item \textbf{Reconstruction algorithm.} Tikhonov-regularised
        pseudoinverse vs.\ NeuroDOT's optimised reconstruction with
        spatiotemporal priors.
  \item \textbf{Coordinate system.} MNI152 vs.\ Talairach, with
        potential residual misalignment at the mesh-to-voxel mapping.
\end{enumerate}

% ============================================================================
\section{Discussion}
% ============================================================================

dot-jax demonstrates that the complete CW-DOT forward--inverse
pipeline can be implemented as a differentiable, JIT-compiled JAX
program. The key contribution is not the reconstruction accuracy ---
which, on a generic atlas with default parameters, cannot match
calibrated subject-specific systems --- but the \textbf{architectural
capability}: every function in the pipeline, from optical property
computation through system matrix assembly to detector value
extraction, is composable with \texttt{jax.grad}, \texttt{jax.vmap},
and \texttt{jax.jit}.

This opens several directions:

\paragraph{Automatic Jacobian verification.}
The adjoint Jacobian formula $J = -\Phi_s \otimes \Phi_d \odot V$
can be independently verified by computing $\partial\hat{y}/\partial\mu_a$
via \texttt{jax.grad} through the full solve. dot-jax is, to our
knowledge, the first DOT package where this cross-check is available.

\paragraph{Gradient-based parameter optimisation.}
Optical properties ($\mu_a$, $\mu_s'$, $n$), regularisation parameters,
and even mesh node positions can be optimised by gradient descent through
the forward model. This is the foundation for learned forward models
and neural-ODE-based reconstruction.

\paragraph{Simulation-based inference for DOT.}
The JIT-compiled forward model can serve as the simulator for
SBI~\citep{Cranmer2020sbi}, enabling amortised posterior estimation
over optical property maps. A single trained neural density estimator
could replace per-subject iterative reconstruction.

\paragraph{Integration with the NeuroJAX ecosystem.}
dot-jax shares the JAX/Equinox/Lineax stack with
vpjax~(hemodynamic modelling), sbi4dwi~(diffusion microstructure),
and neurojax~(whole-brain dynamics). This enables joint forward
models where diffusion-derived tissue structure constrains optical
scattering, hemodynamic models couple to the DOT observation
equation, and neural dynamics drive the chromophore concentration
changes that DOT observes.

\subsection{Limitations}

The current implementation uses dense matrix assembly, limiting
practical mesh sizes to $\sim$10{,}000 nodes on CPU. The sparse CG
solver (\texttt{forward\_cw\_sparse}) extends this to $\sim$100{,}000
nodes on GPU. Subject-specific head meshes from segmented MRI would
substantially improve reconstruction accuracy. The absence of
short-separation channels in the ds004569 cap design limits
superficial signal regression.

